card.statement:
Let $\mathcal{C}$ be an indecomposable set. We say that $\mathcal{C} $ is admissible if it satisfies the following conditions:

- [(i)] For any $1\leq k-1\leq n$, we have $(k, i)\succ_{\mathcal{C}}(k, j)$ only if $i<j$;

- [(ii)] $(n,i)\succeq_{\mathcal{C}} (n,j)$ only if $i<j$;

- [(iii)] There is not cross in $\mathcal{C} $;

- [(iv)] For every $(k,i)\ (k,j)\in\mathfrak{V}(\mathcal{C})$ with $1\leq k\leq n-1$ there exists $s< t$ such that one of the following holds

\begin{equation}
\begin{split}
&\{(k,i)>(k+1,s)\geq (k,j),\ (k,i)\geq (k-1,t)>(k,j)\}\subseteq \mathcal{C},\\
&\{(k,i)>(k+1,s),(k+1,t)\geq (k,j)\}\subseteq  \mathcal{C}.
\end{split}
\end{equation}

An arbitrary set $\mathcal{C}$ is admissible if every indecomposable subset of $\mathcal{C}$ is admissible.
